(in-ns 'go.hash.maphash) (go/file "hasher.go" :imports [])




















































































































(go/type Hasher "A Hasher defines the interface between a hash-based container and its elements.\nIt provides a hash function and an equivalence relation over values\nof type T, enabling those values to be inserted in hash tables\nand similar data structures.\n\nOf course, comparable types can already be used as keys of Go's\nbuilt-in map type, but a Hasher enables non-comparable types to be\nused as keys of a suitable hash table too.\nHashers may be useful even for comparable types, to define an\nequivalence relation that differs from the usual one (==), such as a\nfield-based comparison for a pointer-to-struct type, or a\ncase-insensitive comparison for strings, as in this example:\n\n\t// CaseInsensitive is a Hasher[string] whose\n\t// equivalence relation ignores letter case.\n\ttype CaseInsensitive struct{}\n\n\tfunc (CaseInsensitive) Hash(h *Hash, s string) {\n\t\th.WriteString(strings.ToLower(s))\n\t}\n\n\tfunc (CaseInsensitive) Equal(x, y string) bool {\n\t\t// (We avoid strings.EqualFold as it is not\n\t\t// consistent with ToLower for all values.)\n\t\treturn strings.ToLower(x) == strings.ToLower(y)\n\t}\n\nA Hasher also permits values to be used with other hash-based data\nstructures such as a Bloom filter.\nThe [ComparableHasher] type makes it convenient to enable comparable\ntypes to be used in such data structures under their usual (==)\nequivalence relation.\n\n# Hash invariants\n\nIf two values are equal as defined by Equal(x, y), then they must\nhave the same hash as defined by the effects of Hash(h, x) on h.\n\nHashers must be logically stateless: the behavior of the Hash and\nEqual methods depends only on the arguments.\n\n# Writing a good function\n\nWhen defining a hash function and equivalence relation for a data\ntype, it may help to first define a canonical encoding for values\nof that type as a sequence of elements, each being a number,\nstring, boolean, or pointer.\nAn encoding is canonical if two values that are logically equal\nhave the same encoding, even if they are represented differently.\nFor example, a canonical case-insensitive encoding of a string is\n[strings.ToLower].\n\nOnce you have defined the encoding, the Hasher's Hash method should\nencode a value into the [Hash] using a sequence of calls to\n[Hash.Write] for byte slices, [Hash.WriteString] for strings,\n[Hash.WriteByte] for bytes, and [WriteComparable] for elements of\nother types. The Hasher's Equal method should compute the\nencodings of two values, then compare their corresponding\nelements, returning false at the first mismatch.\n\nA Hash method may discard information so long as it remains\nconsistent with the Equal method as defined above.\nFor example, valid implementations of CaseInsensitive.Hash might inspect\nonly the first letter of the string, or even use a constant value.\nHowever, the lossier the hash function, the more frequent\nthe hash collisions and the slower the hash table.\n\nSome data types, such as sets, are inherently unordered: the set\n{a, b, c} is equal to the set {c, b, a}.\nIn some cases it is possible to define a canonical encoding for a\nset by sorting the elements into some order.\nIn other cases this may inefficient, since it may require allocating\nmemory, or infeasible, as when there is no convenient order.\nAnother way to hash an unordered set is to compute the hash\nfor each element separately, then combine all the element hashes\nusing a commutative (order-independent) operator such as + or ^.\n\nThe Hash method below, for a hypothetical Set type, illustrates\nthis approach:\n\n\ttype Set[T comparable] struct{ ... }\n\n\ttype setHasher[T comparable] struct{}\n\n\tfunc (setHasher[T]) Hash(hash *maphash.Hash, set *Set[T]) {\n\t\tvar accum uint64\n\t\tfor elem := range set.Elements() {\n\t\t\t// Initialize a hasher for the element,\n\t\t\t// using same seed as the outer hash.\n\t\t\tvar sub maphash.Hash\n\t\t\tsub.SetSeed(hash.Seed())\n\n\t\t\t// Hash the element.\n\t\t\tmaphash.WriteComparable(&sub, elem)\n\n\t\t\t// Mix the element's hash into the set's hash.\n\t\t\taccum ^= sub.Sum64()\n\t\t}\n\t\tmaphash.WriteComparable(hash, accum)\n\t}\n\nIn many languages, a data type's hash operation simply returns an\ninteger value.\nHowever, that makes it possible for an adversary to systematically\nconstruct a large number of values that all have the same hash,\ndegrading the asymptotic performance of hash tables in a\ndenial-of-service attack known as \"hash flooding\".\nBy contrast, computing hashes as a sequence of values emitted into\na [Hash] with an unpredictable [Seed] that varies from one hash\ntable to another mitigates this attack.\n\nIn effect, the Seed chooses one of 2⁶⁴ different hash functions.\nThe code example above calls SetSeed on the element's sub-Hasher\nso that it uses the same hash function as for the Set itself, and\nnot a random one.\n" :type-params [T] (interface
    (Hash [(* Hash) T])
    (Equal ^bool [^T x ^:go/grouped ^T y])))









(go/type ComparableHasher "ComparableHasher is an implementation of [Hasher] whose\nEqual(x, y) method is consistent with x == y.\n\nComparableHasher is defined only for comparable types.\nThe type system will not prevent you from instantiating a type\nsuch as ComparableHasher[any]; nonetheless you must not pass\nnon-comparable argument values to its Hash or Equal methods.\n" :type-params [^comparable T] (struct
    ^{:line 135 :tag (array 0 (func [T]))} _)) ; disallow comparison, and conversion between ComparableHasher[X] and ComparableHasher[Y]


^{:go/end 138} (go/method Hash [(ComparableHasher T) ^{:tag (* Hash)} h ^T v] (^{:inst [T]} WriteComparable h v))
^{:go/end 139} (go/method Equal ^bool [(ComparableHasher T) ^T x ^:go/grouped ^T y] (== x y))
